## 3. Humanity Alone Cannot Sustain the Earth

Furthermore, humans are not the only beings that require protection.  
There are people, and thus there are nations. But humanity alone does not constitute the Earth.

There are animals. Plants. Insects that fly, fish that swim, and birds that carry seeds. Beneath the soil, countless microscopic lives work tirelessly.

* Without plants, we cannot breathe.
* Without insects, flowers cannot bear fruit.
* An ocean stripped of fish falls into a quiet of death.
* A land deprived of forests loses its ability to hold water, turning rain from a blessing into a disaster.
* Without water, no living soul survives.
* Without air, neither king nor beggar can draw a single breath.

Human. Animal. Plant. Insect. Fish. Soil. Forest. Ocean. Air. Water.  
These do not exist in isolation. **They are one singular life force breathing through countless forms.**

Humanity is not the ruler of this web of life. We are merely one thread woven into it, sustained by the whole. To believe that humans own the Earth and may consume all other life at will is a grave illusion.

If you pull one thread violently, the entire tapestry unravels. The Earth operates no differently.

The disappearance of a tiny insect, the loss of a single forest, the pollution of a single river—these are not distant, unrelated events. They are individual threads slipping out of the tapestry of life.

Ultimately, the very fabric supporting humanity itself will be lost.

If people disappear, nations cease to exist. And if non-human life, water, air, forests, and oceans are lost, humanity cannot endure. An Earth stripped of all life would revert to a cold, lifeless rock—devoid of borders, history, or prayer.

